{
 "lead": "Quantization rounds each sample to one of L allowed levels, so a sample becomes one of a finite set of values. A uniform quantizer spaces the levels equally, so a quiet signal gets the same absolute error as a loud one and a much worse signal-to-noise ratio. Speech is mostly quiet, so telephony gives small samples fine steps and large samples coarse steps; companding is the practical way to do this. Telephone PCM and VoIP gateways still use the μ-law and A-law curves.",
 "prereq": "You need: <a href=\"#sampling-theorem-aliasing-and-reconstruction\">sampling</a>, and mean-square value and variance (see Probability and random variables review).",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Uniform quantization and its noise",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "A uniform quantizer covering −V to +V with L = 2ⁿ levels, for an n-bit code, has <b>step size</b> Δ = 2V/L. Each sample x is replaced by the nearest level q(x); the <b>quantization error</b> e = q(x) − x lies between −Δ/2 and +Δ/2 while |x| ≤ V. A sample beyond ±V is clipped to the end level, which is <b>overload</b> distortion."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "For a busy signal and a fine step, e is equally likely anywhere in one step, with density 1/Δ. Its mean is zero and its mean square is ∫ e²(1/Δ) de from −Δ/2 to Δ/2 = Δ²/12. This granular noise is present even on a perfect channel."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "P<sub>q</sub> = Δ²/12,   Δ = 2V/2ⁿ",
     "where": "P<sub>q</sub> is the quantization noise power in V² (into 1 Ω), V the peak of the range and n the bits per sample."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The <b>signal-to-quantization-noise ratio</b> is SQNR = P<sub>x</sub>/P<sub>q</sub>, with P<sub>x</sub> the mean-square signal. Substituting Δ gives SQNR = 3·2²ⁿ·P<sub>x</sub>/V², or in decibels"
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "SQNR = 4.77 + 6.02n − 20 log(V/σ) dB",
     "where": "σ = √P<sub>x</sub> is the rms value of the signal and V/σ its <b>loading factor</b>. For a full-scale sine, V/σ = √2 and the rule becomes the familiar <b>6.02n + 1.76 dB</b>. For speech peaks held at 4σ, it becomes 6.02n − 7.27 dB. Each extra bit adds 6.02 dB; halving the signal costs 6.02 dB."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Non-uniform quantization and companding",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "A <b>non-uniform quantizer</b> uses small steps near zero and large steps at large |x|, so the error grows with the signal and SQNR stays nearly constant. The usual way to build one is <b>companding</b>: a <b>compressor</b> y = C(x) lifts small inputs, a uniform quantizer follows, and an <b>expander</b> C⁻¹ at the receiver undoes the curve. Near zero the compressor slope C′(0) divides the effective step by that factor. Telephone codecs build the smooth curves from 8 straight segments per polarity."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "y = ln(1 + μ|x|/V) / ln(1 + μ)",
     "where": "This is the <b>μ-law</b> for x ≥ 0 (odd symmetry for x &lt; 0), with y the compressed output normalised to 1. μ = 255 is the standard value, used in North America and Japan. Its slope at zero is μ/ln(1 + μ) = 255/5.545 = 46.0, a 33.3 dB gain for small signals."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The <b>A-law</b>, standard in Europe, India and on international links, is linear for small inputs and logarithmic above: y = A(|x|/V)/(1 + ln A) for |x|/V ≤ 1/A, and y = [1 + ln(A|x|/V)]/(1 + ln A) for 1/A ≤ |x|/V ≤ 1, with A = 87.6, so 1/A = 0.0114 and 1 + ln A = 5.473. Its small-signal slope is 87.6/5.473 = 16.0, or 24.1 dB. The two laws are not compatible, so calls between the regions are converted. Some syllabi call non-uniform quantization logarithmic quantization."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "What companding buys and what it cannot do",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "For large inputs (μ|x| ≫ V) the μ-law quantization noise becomes proportional to signal power, and SQNR ≈ 3L²/[ln(1 + μ)]². With 8 bits that is 3 × 65 536/30.75 = 6394, or 38.1 dB, whatever the level. Uniform 8-bit PCM gives 49.9 dB at full scale but only 9.9 dB for a sine 40 dB down; μ-law gives about 34 dB there. Companding trades about 12 dB at full scale for a flat SQNR over a 40 dB range of talkers."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Companding cannot stop overload: a sample beyond ±V is still clipped. The noise left during silence, <b>idle-channel noise</b>, is reduced by the fine steps near zero but not removed. And Δ²/12 is quantization noise only; thermal noise and bit errors add their own powers."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "examples": [
  {
   "question": "An 8-bit uniform quantizer has a range of ±1 V and a full-scale sinusoidal input. Find L, Δ, P<sub>q</sub> and the SQNR in dB, and check the 6.02n + 1.76 rule.",
   "steps": [
    "L = 2⁸ = 256 levels.",
    "Δ = 2V/L = 2 V/256 = 0.0078125 V = 7.8125 mV.",
    "P<sub>q</sub> = Δ²/12 = (7.8125 × 10⁻³)²/12 = 6.1035 × 10⁻⁵/12 = 5.086 × 10⁻⁶ V².",
    "P<sub>x</sub> = V²/2 = 0.5 V², so SQNR = 0.5/5.086 × 10⁻⁶ = 98 304 = 1.5 × 256².",
    "10 log<sub>10</sub>(98 304) = 49.93 dB; the rule gives 6.02 × 8 + 1.76 = 49.92 dB."
   ],
   "answer": "L = 256, Δ = 7.8125 mV, P<sub>q</sub> = 5.086 × 10⁻⁶ V², SQNR = 49.9 dB."
  },
  {
   "question": "For μ-law (μ = 255) and A-law (A = 87.6), find the compressed output y for normalised inputs |x|/V = 0.01, 0.1 and 0.5, and find how many times finer the effective step is near zero than the uniform step.",
   "dry_run": {
    "head": [
     "|x|/V",
     "μ-law y",
     "A-law y",
     "A-law segment"
    ],
    "rows": [
     [
      "0.01",
      "ln(3.55)/5.545 = 0.229",
      "87.6 × 0.01/5.473 = 0.160",
      "linear (0.01 &lt; 0.0114)"
     ],
     [
      "0.1",
      "ln(26.5)/5.545 = 0.591",
      "(1 + ln 8.76)/5.473 = 0.579",
      "log"
     ],
     [
      "0.5",
      "ln(128.5)/5.545 = 0.876",
      "(1 + ln 43.8)/5.473 = 0.873",
      "log"
     ]
    ]
   },
   "steps": [
    "μ-law: ln(1 + μ) = ln 256 = 5.545. For 0.01: 1 + 2.55 = 3.55 and ln 3.55 = 1.267, so y = 1.267/5.545 = 0.229. The table repeats this for 0.1 and 0.5.",
    "A-law: 1/A = 0.0114 and 1 + ln 87.6 = 5.473. 0.01 is below 0.0114, so the linear part applies: y = 0.876/5.473 = 0.160. 0.1 and 0.5 use the log part.",
    "Near zero the effective step is Δ divided by the compressor slope: Δ/46.0 for μ-law and Δ/16.0 for A-law.",
    "With the 8-bit ±1 V quantizer above, Δ = 7.81 mV becomes 0.170 mV (μ-law) and 0.488 mV (A-law) for the smallest inputs."
   ],
   "answer": "A 1 % input is lifted to 23 % (μ-law) or 16 % (A-law) of full scale; small signals see steps 46 and 16 times finer."
  }
 ],
 "traps": [
  "6.02n + 1.76 dB holds only for a full-scale sine. For any other signal use 4.77 + 6.02n − 20 log(V/σ).",
  "Δ = 2V/L uses the full peak-to-peak range; V/L is half the true step.",
  "Companding does not remove overload; samples beyond ±V are clipped either way.",
  "μ-law and A-law are compressors at the transmitter; the expander is their inverse at the receiver. μ = 255 and A = 87.6 are not interchangeable."
 ],
 "quiz": [
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "How many levels does an n-bit uniform quantizer have, and what is its maximum error?",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "L = 2ⁿ levels; the maximum error inside the range is Δ/2, with Δ = 2V/L."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "Why does non-uniform quantization suit speech better than uniform quantization?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "Speech is small most of the time. Fixed steps give quiet passages a poor SQNR; fine steps near zero and coarse steps at the peaks make the error follow the signal, so SQNR stays nearly level."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "A 10-bit uniform quantizer has a ±2 V range and a full-scale sine input. Find Δ, P<sub>q</sub> and the SQNR.",
   "marks": 4,
   "a": "L = 1024; Δ = 4 V/1024 = 3.906 mV; P<sub>q</sub> = Δ²/12 = 1.272 × 10⁻⁶ V²; SQNR = 2 V²/1.272 × 10⁻⁶ = 1 572 864 → 61.97 dB (rule: 61.96 dB)."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "Speech with peaks at 4σ is quantized with 8 bits. Find the SQNR.",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "SQNR = 4.77 + 6.02 × 8 − 20 log 4 = 4.77 + 48.16 − 12.04 = 40.9 dB."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "What happens to the SQNR of a uniform quantizer if the step is doubled for the same signal?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "P<sub>q</sub> = Δ²/12 becomes four times larger, so SQNR falls by 10 log 4 = 6.02 dB, the same as losing one bit."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "A loud peak exceeds the full-scale range of a companded PCM system. Does companding prevent the distortion?",
   "marks": 4,
   "a": "No. Companding only reshapes the steps inside ±V; any sample beyond ±V is clipped to the end level, which is overload distortion."
  }
 ],
 "beyond": "Not for the exam. G.711 μ-law and A-law still carry speech between telephone networks and VoIP gateways at 64 kbit/s. Modern codecs such as Opus use prediction and transform coding instead of a fixed curve. Search next for vector quantization and Lloyd–Max quantizers.",
